/-!
# Testing J-2 on module-finite algebras

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PC, lemma-J-2, implication (3) to (1),
with J-1 and J-2 expanded by Tag 07P7, definition-J
(blueprint: lem-per-j2-finite-algebra-criterion).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

/-- If every module-finite algebra over a Noetherian commutative ring has open regular
locus, then every finite type algebra does. This is the J-2 clause in `IsQuasiExcellent`.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PC, lemma-J-2, implication (3) to (1),
and Tag 07P7, definition-J (blueprint: lem-per-j2-finite-algebra-criterion).

Proof (the six steps of the node):
1. Fix a prime `p` of `R` and a finite purely inseparable extension `L` of its residue
   field `k`. Set `A = R/p`. The fraction description in
   `def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime` identifies `k` with the fraction field of `A`:
   the residue fraction `r/s` maps to `(r mod p)/(s mod p)`. Both equality relations
   are cross multiplication modulo `p`, because primality cancels multipliers outside
   `p`. Thus `A` embeds in `L`.
2. In characteristic zero, pure inseparability identifies `L` with `k`; take `B = A`.
   In positive characteristic `p`, choose a finite `k`-basis `alpha_i` of `L`. Write
   `alpha_i^(p^e_i) = c_i/d_i` in the fraction field of `A`, with `d_i` nonzero.
   Set `beta_i = d_i * alpha_i`. Then, with `n_i = p^e_i`, the explicit identity
   `beta_i^n_i = c_i * d_i^(n_i - 1)` is a monic relation over `A`, also when `n_i = 1`.
   This uses `def-weil-pbc-finite-purely-inseparable-extension`.
3. In positive characteristic take `B = A[beta_i]` inside `L`. Reducing powers by those
   monic relations shows that the finitely many monomials with exponents below `n_i`
   span `B` over `A`, hence over `R`. This is `def-weil-finitely-generated-module`.
   The fractions of `B` contain `k` and every `alpha_i = beta_i/d_i`, hence exhaust `L`.
   In both characteristics `B` is a domain and a module-finite `R`-algebra.
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-noetherian`, `B` is Noetherian. Apply the hypothesis
   to `B` to obtain the open subset of primes where its localizations are regular local.
5. This open is nonempty: the zero prime of the domain `B` has localization `L`.
   A field is Noetherian local, has dimension zero, and its zero maximal ideal is
   generated by the empty family. It is therefore regular local by
   `def-weil-regular-scheme`. No reducedness assumption on `R` has been used.
6. The constructed `B` satisfies every hypothesis of `lem-exc-j2-residue-field-criterion`:
   it is an `R`-subalgebra of the specified `L`, module-finite, has fraction field `L`,
   and has a nonempty regular open. That criterion gives the asserted open regular
   locus for every finite type algebra, exactly J-2 in `def-exc-excellent-ring`.
   For the zero ring there are no primes, so the same criterion applies vacuously.
-/
theorem isOpen_regularLocus_of_finite_algebras
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (hfinite : ∀ (B : Type (max u v)) [CommRing B] [Algebra R B] [Module.Finite R B],
      IsOpen {q : PrimeSpectrum B | IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q.asIdeal)})
    (C : Type (max u v)) [CommRing C] [Algebra R C] [Algebra.FiniteType R C] :
    IsOpen {q : PrimeSpectrum C | IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q.asIdeal)} := by
  sorry
